**Diary — 2026-03-18 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto ROCm/HIP targeting **gfx906** (Vega 10 / MI60-class). The goal was not a full rewrite, just a clean port of the hot path: kernel launches, memory management, streams/events, and the main compute kernels.

---

### 1. Build setup

I added a HIP target to the CMake build:

```cmake
find_package(hip REQUIRED)

target_link_libraries(engine PRIVATE hip::device)
target_compile_options(engine PRIVATE
    $<BUILD_INTERFACE:hip::device>
)
```

For direct compilation I used:

```bash
hipcc -O3 -march=gfx906 -std=c++17 \
  -Iinclude \
  src/engine_hip.cpp \
  -o engine_hip
```

Important: I explicitly targeted **gfx906** from the start. If you let it default to a newer arch, you get surprises at runtime.

---

### 2. Runtime API mapping

The CUDA runtime calls mapped almost 1:1:

```cpp
// CUDA
cudaMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
cudaMemcpy(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
cudaMemset(d_y, 0, n * sizeof(float));
cudaFree(d_x);

// HIP
hipMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
hipMemcpy(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), hipMemcpyHostToDevice);
hipMemset(d_y, 0, n * sizeof(float));
hipFree(d_x);
```

Streams and events:

```cpp
hipStream_t stream;
hipEvent_t start, stop;

hipStreamCreate(&stream);
hipEventCreate(&start);
hipEventCreate(&stop);

hipEventRecord(start, stream);
kernel<<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(...);
hipEventRecord(stop, stream);
hipEventSynchronize(stop);

float ms = 0.0f;
hipEventElapsedTime(&ms, start, stop);
```

